  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA strong upper high remains in place across the Central Plains, \nsupporting our current heat wave. This morning's TOP sounding showed \n500 mb heights of 597 dam, not too far off max climatological \nvalues. Surface moisture also continues to be deeper and more \nresistant than guidance was depicting a few days ago, with \nwidespread upper 70s dewpoints still in place across most of eastern \nKS. As such, hot and humid conditions are expected to stay in place \ntoday, with heat indices climbing above 110 in most places. Do \nexpect slightly deeper mixing tomorrow as winds turn a bit more \nsouthwesterly, so we should see our warmest day of the stretch but \nwith dewpoints a few degrees lower. Regardless, heat indices should \nclimb near or above 110 degrees area-wide, so extended the Extreme \nHeat Warning for one more day.\n\nBy Tuesday, the upper high will begin to weaken and shift westward \nas a shortwave trough drops southeast over the Great Lakes. This \nwill also help push a weak cold front southward across the area. \nParticularly with potential for some scattered showers and \nthunderstorms behind the front, this will help temperatures cool \ndown compared to the prior few days. Confidence in these cooler \ntemperatures has increased enough to trim the Extreme Heat Watch out \nof northern Kansas, but have kept it in place across east-central \nKS. May be able to cancel this as well if confidence further \nincreases in clouds/rain behind the front.\n\nWednesday into Thursday will continue to see low rain chances as \nanother weak mid-level perturbation increases lift north of \nTuesday's frontal boundary. Temperatures will continue to be \ndependent on coverage of rain and clouds, likely staying in the 80s \nwith more clouds and rising into the low/mid 90s with more sun. But \noverall, does look less oppressive in terms of heat index compared \nto today and Monday. Good indication in ensemble guidance for a \nstronger shortwave to drop southeast across the Midwest late Thursday\ninto early Friday. This should bring our best chance for scattered \nshowers and storms, followed by a more notable cold front. Behind the\nfront high temperatures in the 80s seem probable, along with at \nleast a modest drop in dewpoints.",
